  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 240 PM CDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\nMonday, an upper-level trough will become established across the \nwestern United States, with an embedded upper-level low ejecting \nnortheastward through the afternoon and evening. Increasing flow \naloft and large-scale ascent associated with the approaching trough \nwill overspread the region. Warm air advection will also strengthen \nthrough the day as southerly flow increases within the low and mid-\nlevels. At the surface, low pressure will deepen across the northern \nPlains, with a secondary low developing across eastern Colorado. An \nassociated cold front will advance southeast into the area Monday \nafternoon. Ahead of the front, temperatures should warm quickly into \nthe lower to middle 80s. Some differences remain among model \nguidance regarding the speed of the cold front, resulting in \nuncertainty regarding precipitation chances. A faster frontal \nprogression would limit the amount of time for moisture return and \nstronger forcing to overlap across the area, resulting in lower \nprecipitation chances. Current thinking favors this somewhat faster \nprogression, though trends will continue to be monitored. Behind the \nfront, northwest winds will become gusty. However, minimum relative \nhumidity values are expected to remain above 30 percent, keeping \noverall fire weather concerns fairly low.\n\nTuesday, the strong early-season cold front will finish moving \nthrough the area during the early morning hours, with considerably \nstronger mid-level cold air advection developing behind it. The \nfront is then expected to stall across Kansas, keeping the forecast \narea firmly within the cooler air mass. High temperatures are \ncurrently forecast to remain in the middle to upper 60s, with \nlingering cloud cover potentially limiting daytime heating. With the \nfrontal boundary remaining south of the area, the better \nprecipitation chances should also remain across Kansas. Tuesday \nnight will be quite cool, with lows falling into the middle to upper \n40s across northern portions of the area and lower to middle 50s \nelsewhere.\n\nWednesday through late next week, the active pattern will persist \nwith westerly to southwesterly flow aloft and multiple weak mid-\nlevel disturbances moving through the region within the cooler \nregime. Periodic precipitation chances will remain possible, though \nconfidence in the timing and coverage of individual disturbances \nremains limited. Highs should generally remain in the lower to \nmiddle 70s, with overnight lows in the upper 40s to lower 50s.\n\nBeyond this period, model guidance suggests another potentially \nstrong upper-level trough may approach from the north next weekend \ninto early the following week. This could reinforce the cooler air \nmass and provide additional precipitation chances. Confidence in the \ntiming and magnitude of this system remains low at this range, but \nthe overall pattern favors a transition toward more fall-like \nconditions through next weekend.",
